<article class="env-lemma" id="00W7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00W7">Lemma <span data-tag="00W7">7.10.6</span>.</a> Any two morphisms $f, g: \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{V}$ of coverings inducing the same morphism $U \to V$ induce the same map $H^0(\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{F}) \to H^0(\mathcal{U}, \mathcal{F})$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\mathcal{U} = \{ U_ i \to U\} _{i\in I}$ and $\mathcal{V} = \{ V_ j \to V\} _{j\in J}$. The morphism $f$ consists of a map $U\to V$, a map $\alpha : I \to J$ and maps $f_ i : U_ i \to V_{\alpha (i)}$. Likewise, $g$&#160;determines a map $\beta : I \to J$ and maps $g_ i : U_ i \to V_{\beta (i)}$. As $f$ and $g$ induce the same map $U\to V$, the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ &  V_{\alpha (i)} \ar[dr] \\  U_ i \ar[ur]^{f_ i} \ar[dr]_{g_ i} &  &  V \\  &  V_{\beta (i)} \ar[ur] }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is commutative for every $i\in I$. Hence $f$ and $g$ factor through the fibre product 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ &  V_{\alpha (i)} \\  U_ i \ar[r]^-\varphi \ar[ur]^{f_ i} \ar[dr]_{g_ i} &  V_{\alpha (i)} \times _ V V_{\beta (i)} \ar[u]_{\text{pr}_1} \ar[d]^{\text{pr}_2} \\  &  V_{\beta (i)}. }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Now let $s = (s_ j)_ j \in H^0(\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{F})$. Then for all $i\in I$: 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  (f^*s)_ i = f_ i^*(s_{\alpha (i)}) = \varphi ^*\text{pr}_1^*(s_{\alpha (i)}) = \varphi ^*\text{pr}_2^*(s_{\beta (i)}) = g_ i^*(s_{\beta (i)}) = (g^*s)_ i,  \]
</div>
    <p>
       where the middle equality is given by the definition of $H^0(\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{F})$. This shows that the maps $H^0(\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{F}) \to H^0(\mathcal{U}, \mathcal{F})$ induced by $f$ and $g$ are equal. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>